WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.720
A Gaussian integer is a plus b
i, where a and b are integers.

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:07.020
Modulo one plus i,

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:11.940
two numbers agree when their difference is
a Gaussian integer multiple of one plus i.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:18.340
To test divisibility, multiply
by one minus i over one minus i.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:20.770
The denominator becomes two.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:26.850
Collect real and imaginary parts:
the quantity a plus b divided by two,

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:32.430
plus i times the quantity
b minus a divided by two.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:37.180
Both halves are integers exactly
when a and b have the same parity.

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:41.220
Two even integers work,
and two odd integers work.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.920
Different parities give
two noninteger halves.

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:49.800
Thus one plus i divides a plus b
i exactly when a plus b is even.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:52.860
There are only two residues.

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:57.555
Send a plus b i to a plus b modulo two.

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:01.380
For three plus i, the sum is
four, giving residue zero.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:06.460
For three plus two i, the sum
is five, giving residue one.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.960
The output records arithmetic
parity, not a complex angle.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:16.060
This map preserves addition. It
preserves multiplication too:

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:19.700
the real part of a
product is a c minus b d,

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:23.940
and the imaginary part is
a d plus b c. Modulo two,

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:29.260
minus and plus agree, so their sum is
the product of a plus b and c plus d.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.660
The map is onto the two-element field,

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:35.440
and its kernel consists exactly
of multiples of one plus i.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:41.710
In particular, one, negative one, i,
and negative i all have residue one.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.740
The factor identities
explain the key congruences:

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:49.900
i minus one equals i times
the quantity one plus i,

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:55.840
and two equals the product of one plus i
and one minus i. Both signs become one.

00:01:56.440 --> 00:02:00.860
For any positive integer u, apply the
map to each polynomial coefficient.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:06.180
The powers of P and the relation P to
the u squared equals one remain in place.

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:12.720
Only the coefficients change. For
example, i minus P reduces to one plus P.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:18.890
This equality also respects the
cycle relation, even when u is one.

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:23.860
Keep the order of operations
clear. In defining E of P,

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:27.980
first form the exact integral
quotients supplied by the common signs.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:30.160
Only then reduce their coefficients.

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:34.700
Both two and one plus i
become zero in the quotient,

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:39.100
so neither can be used as a denominator
there. We carry forward two facts:

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:43.400
multiples of i minus one disappear,
and both signs have residue one.
